# Go KV Store
A simple in-memory key-value store written in Go as part of my Go learning journey.
 
## Features
 
- Set, get, and delete values
- HTTP API interface
- TCP plain-text protocol interface
- Both interfaces share the same underlying store
- Concurrency safe (read/write mutex)
- Unit and integration tests
 
## Running
 
```bash
go run main.go
```
 
Both servers start simultaneously. By default:
- HTTP on port `8080`
- TCP on port `9090`
 
Ports can be overridden with flags:
 
```bash
go run main.go -http :8080 -tcp :9090
```
 
---
 
## HTTP API
 
### Health check
 
```
GET /health
```
 
Returns `200 OK`.
 
### Get a value
 
```
GET /kv/{key}
```
 
Returns `200 OK` with the value as plain text, or `404 Not Found` if the key does not exist.
 
### Set / update a value
 
```
PUT /kv/{key}
Content-Type: application/json
 
{"value": "your-value"}
```
 
Returns `204 No Content` on success.
 
### Delete a value
 
```
DELETE /kv/{key}
```
 
Returns `204 No Content` (idempotent — deleting a missing key also returns 204).
 
### HTTP Example
 
```bash
# Set a value
curl -X PUT http://localhost:8080/kv/name \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"value":"Ben"}'
 
# Get a value
curl http://localhost:8080/kv/name
 
# Delete a value
curl -X DELETE http://localhost:8080/kv/name
```
 
---
 
## TCP Protocol
 
The TCP server listens for plain-text, newline-delimited commands. Each command returns a single line response.
 
### Commands
 
| Command | Description | Response |
|---|---|---|
| `GET <key>` | Retrieve a value | The value, or `NOT FOUND` |
| `SET <key> <value>` | Store a value (value may contain spaces) | `OK` |
| `DELETE <key>` | Remove a key | `OK` |
| `HELP` | List available commands | Command summary |
 
Errors are returned as `ERR <message>`.
 
### TCP Example
 
Connect with `nc` or `telnet`:
 
```bash
nc localhost 9090
```
 
Then send commands:
 
```
SET name Ben
OK
GET name
Ben
DELETE name
OK
GET name
NOT FOUND
HELP
Commands: GET <key> | SET <key> <value> | DELETE <key> | HELP
```
 
